Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action; cold northern light; Buck a massive mixed St. Bernard–shepherd, one hundred forty pounds, rich brown coat with white chest splash and brown muzzle markings — identity locked for the whole film

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE - SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Sun-kissed lawns, gravel drive, wide veranda half hidden among poplars. BUCK, a great brown dog with white chest splash, rules the demesne — plunging in the cement tank, escorting children, stalking among fox terriers like a king.

(SOUND: birdsong, distant laughter, water splash)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog from Puget Sound to San Diego. Men had found a yellow metal in the Northland. They wanted dogs.

EXT. ORCHARD AND COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, leads Buck through the orchard on what the dog imagines is a stroll. At the little flag station a STRANGER waits. Money chinks between them.

(SOUND: coins clink, night insects, distant train whistle)

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles stout rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck accepts the rope with quiet dignity — until the ends pass into the stranger's hands. He growls. The rope tightens. He springs; the stranger throws him; breath shuts off. Eyes glaze.

(SOUND: rope cinch, Buck's choked fury, train flagged)

They fling him into the baggage car.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR AND SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT SHED - NIGHT

Jolting dark. Locomotive shrieks a crossing. Buck's jaws close on the kidnapper's hand until he is choked senseless again.

(SOUND: train roar, struggle, mangled cry)

In a shed behind a saloon the kidnapper's hand is wrapped in bloody cloth. The SALOON-KEEPER peers in by tallow candle. They file off Buck's brass collar and fling him into a cagelike crate.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He could not understand what it all meant. He felt oppressed by the vague sense of impending calamity.

EXT. SEATTLE HIGH-WALLED BACK YARD - DAY

Four men carry the crate in. The MAN IN RED SWEATER — stout, sweater sagging at the neck — signs the book, brings hatchet and club.

(SOUND: hatchet splitting wood, dogs snarling, men shouting from the wall)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck launches one hundred forty pounds of fury. The club checks him mid-air. Again and again he charges; again and again the club smashes him down. Blood from nose and mouth. A final shrewd blow — he crumples senseless.

(SOUND: club impacts, Buck's lionlike roar, body hitting earth)

He comes to. The man pats the head he pounded.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

Water. Raw meat from the man's hand. Buck drinks and eats. Beaten — not broken.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He saw, once for all, that he stood no chance against a man with a club. That club was his introduction to the reign of primitive law.

Other dogs come and pass under the same dominion. Money changes hands. A little weazened man — PERRAULT — eyes Buck.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that. Seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

Seattle falls away. Buck and good-natured CURLY, a Newfoundland, stand with PERRAULT and the black-faced giant FRANCOIS. Cold deepens day by day.

(SOUND: propeller throb, wind, gulls fading)

Morning. Propeller quiet. Excitement. Francois leashes them on deck. Buck's feet sink into white mush. He snorts, sniffs, licks — it bites like fire and is gone.

(SOUND: men laughing uproariously)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DAY

Nightmare of confusion. Savages of club and fang. Curly, friendly, advances to a husky. A leap, a metallic clip of teeth — her face ripped open. Thirty huskies close the silent circle. She goes down. They bury her, screaming, beneath the mass.

(SOUND: teeth clip, Curly's agony, snarling mass, axe and clubs scattering dogs)

Francois swings an axe; men with clubs scatter them. Curly lies limp in bloody snow. SPITZ, snow-white, runs out his scarlet tongue and laughs. From that moment Buck hates him with deathless hatred.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you.

Francois fastens harness on Buck. Hauling firewood. Dave the wheeler nips his hindquarters; Spitz jerks the traces. Buck learns "ho," "mush," the swing of bends.

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. NORTHLAND TRAIL AND CAMPS - DAY AND NIGHT

Montage, tight: nine dogs in harness, Spitz leading, Buck between Dave and Sol-leks. Breaking trail in dark, camping after dark. Pound and a half of salmon. Buck learns to eat fast, to steal cunningly when hunger compels.

(SOUND: sled runners, whip crack, wind, dogs panting, ice underfoot)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Under the law of club and fang, whoso took moral considerations into account was a fool. The domesticated generations fell from him. On still cold nights he pointed his nose at a star and howled long and wolflike.

Spitz bullies; Buck avoids the open fight, bides his time with primitive patience. At Lake Le Barge starving huskies raid the camp — pandemonium, blood, Spitz's treacherous side attack. Dolly goes mad; Francois's axe ends her; Spitz rips Buck until the lash drives him off.

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. Some dam fine day heem get mad lak hell an' den heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow. Sure. I know.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK BED - NIGHT

Moonlight. Snowshoe rabbit. The whole team and fifty police huskies in full cry. Buck leads, sounding the old wolf-cry — ecstasy of the kill. Spitz cuts across, breaks the rabbit's back. Buck drives in upon him. To the death.

(SOUND: pack in cry, rabbit shriek, then sudden ghostly quiet — only panting and frost-breath)

They circle. Fang clashes fang. Spitz practised, cold. Buck streaming blood. The silent wolfish circle waits. Buck rushes low — teeth close on Spitz's left foreleg. Bone crunches. Then the right. Spitz on three legs. The circle tightens.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck was inexorable. Mercy was a thing reserved for gentler climes.

Shoulder meets shoulder. The dark circle becomes a dot on the moon-flooded snow as Spitz disappears. Buck stands over the kill — the dominant primordial beast.

(SOUND: final crunch, pack's hell's chorus of delight, then wind over snow)

EXT. CAMP AND RIVER TRAIL - DAWN

Francois finds Spitz missing, Buck covered with wounds.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? I spik true w'en I say dat Buck two devils.

FRANCOIS (CONT'D)
An' now we make good time. No more Spitz, no more trouble, sure.

Francois tries Sol-leks at lead. Buck drives Sol-leks back, stands in the place. Club raised — Buck circles out of range, wise in clubs. An hour of revolt. At last Francois throws down the club. Buck trotts in, laughing triumphantly, swings to the head of the team.

(SOUND: traces snapping home, sled breaking out, runners on ice)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At a bound Buck took up the duties of leadership. The team recovered its old-time solidarity and leaped as one dog in the traces.

Record run — forty miles a day. Skaguay. Francois weeps, arms around Buck. Then they pass out of his life. Mail train. Heavy toil. Dave, sick unto death, bites through traces to die in harness. A revolver shot behind the timber. Buck knows.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET AND TRAIL - DAY

Worn to one hundred fifteen pounds. Sold for a song to HAL — callow, belted with cartridges — CHARLES, watery-eyed, and MERCEDES, Charles's wife. Slipshod camp. Overloaded sled. Outside dogs added. Whip and club. Dog-food gone. Dub shot. Billee knocked on the head with an axe. Skeletons in the traces.

(SOUND: whip whistling, Mercedes weeping, sled runners dragging, Hal swearing)

HAL
Mush on, you lazy brutes!

They stagger into JOHN THORNTON's camp at the mouth of White River. Thornton whittles an axe-handle. Hal will go on over rotten ice.

JOHN THORNTON
The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. Only fools could have made it. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

Buck will not rise. Club falls. Thornton springs — a cry more animal than human — hurls Hal backward.

(SOUND: club on flesh, Thornton's inarticulate cry, Mercedes scream, knife knocked away)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

He cuts Buck's traces. The sled crawls out on the ice. A section gives way. Dogs and humans disappear into a yawning hole.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

(SOUND: ice cracking, distant scream cut off, river hush)

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP ON THE RIVER BANK - DAY

Long spring days. Buck wins back strength. SKEET the setter washes his wounds; NIG the big black dog bounds in play. Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his hands, shakes him, murmurs love-oaths.

(SOUND: river run, birds, Thornton's low laughing curses, Buck's ecstatic whine)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. This man had saved his life; but further, he was the ideal master.

Buck will not let Thornton from his sight — yet deep in the forest a call sounds, mysteriously thrilling. Love draws him back to the fire.

EXT. CLIFF CREST AND FORTY-MILE RAPIDS - DAY

Thornton, on a whim at a three-hundred-foot drop:

JOHN THORNTON
Jump, Buck!

Buck leaps; men drag them back from the edge.

PETE
It's uncanny.

JOHN THORNTON
No, it is splendid, and it is terrible, too.

Later — poling-boat wrecked in rapids. Thornton flung into wild water. Buck swims, feels the hand on his tail, is ordered ashore, returns with the rope, strikes Thornton like a battering ram. Three broken ribs. They camp till he heals.

(SOUND: roar of rapids, shouted "Go, Buck! Go!", rope snubbing, choking gasps)

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON AND STREET - DAY

Sixty below. Crowds. MATTHEWSON's sack of gold on the bar.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't start a thousand pounds.

Thornton borrows from O'Brien. Sled frozen fast — twenty sacks of flour. Odds three to one against. Thornton kneels, cheek to cheek.

JOHN THORNTON
(whispering)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

(SOUND: crowd murmur dying to absolute cold silence)

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck plunges right — crackling under runners.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Left — sled broken out.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws himself forward. Muscles knot under silk fur. Inch by inch — then steady. One hundred yards. Roar of the crowd. Thornton on his knees, shaking Buck, cursing him lovingly. Offers of a thousand, twelve hundred —

JOHN THORNTON
Sir, you can go to hell, sir.

(SOUND: sled runners grating free, crowd explosion, hats in air)

EXT. EASTERN WILDERNESS AND SPRUCE-BOUGH CAMP - DAY AND NIGHT

Lost-mine journey. Gold like yellow butter in the pans. Buck muses by the fire — visions of the short-legged hairy man. The call grows. A timber wolf — long, lean. They sniff noses, run side by side. Buck remembers Thornton and turns back. The wild brother howls mournfully.

(SOUND: lonely howl fading, fire crackle, wind in spruce)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thornton alone held him. The rest of mankind was as nothing.

Buck kills a great bull moose after four days' patience. Returning, he smells calamity. Nig dead with an arrow. Hans feathered like a porcupine. YEEHAT warriors dance about the wreckage. Buck becomes a live hurricane — throats opened, panic, Evil Spirit. Pete dead in his blankets. Skeet at the pool's edge. Thornton in the pool — no trace leads away.

(SOUND: Yeehat chant cut by Buck's fearful roaring, screams, flight through trees)

All day Buck broods by the pool. Night. Full moon. From far away, sharp yelps — the pack. They pour into the moonlight. Buck breaks the boldest neck, slashes others, comes to bay against the gravel bank. Then the long lean wild brother advances. They touch noses. An old wolf howls. Buck howls. He runs with them, side by side with the wild brother.

(SOUND: pack yelping in chorus, then the long wolf howl taken up by Buck)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken. Man and the claims of man no longer bound him.

EXT. OPEN VALLEY AMONG THE TREES - NIGHT

Years later. A great, gloriously coated wolf — brown on muzzle, white rift down the chest — crosses alone into the clearing. Yellow stream from rotted moose-hide sacks sinks into the ground. He muses, howls once long and mournfully, and departs.

(SOUND: one mournful howl, then wind and silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he is not always alone. When the long winter nights come on, he may be seen running at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight or glimmering borealis, leaping gigantic above his fellows, his great throat a-bellow as he sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

In the pale light the Ghost Dog leads them — and the song rises, wild and free.

(MUSIC: lone wolf howl swelling into full pack song under aurora)

FADE OUT.

THE END
